{
  "technical_and_design_decisions": [
    "Implement in Go as an extension of the existing Hyperledger Fabric channel configuration (configtx) API library, following the existing package layout and coding conventions; no changes to Fabric peer/orderer core.",
    "All new functions operate on and/or return protobuf-based configuration objects (cb.Config / config transaction / ConfigEnvelope) so that calls can be chained: a modified config transaction can be passed to further update functions as required by U2-U6.",
    "U1 retrieval functions return a typed config.Organization struct (name, policies, MSP, anchor peers for application orgs, orderer endpoints for orderer orgs) that can be mutated and passed back to existing org-update functions.",
    "Policies added/removed in U2-U4 are represented by a Policy struct supporting signature and implicit-meta types, with conversion helpers between the typed representation and cb.Policy / cb.SignaturePolicyEnvelope / cb.ImplicitMetaPolicy; add/remove is implemented by inserting/deleting entries in the target group's Policies map with the appropriate mod_policy.",
    "U5/U6 build a complete org ConfigGroup (MSP value, policies, endpoints/anchor peers) from an Organization argument and attach it to the Orderer or Application group with a sensible default mod_policy (Admins), returning the mutated config transaction.",
    "U7 unmarshals the org's MSP value (msp.MSPConfig wrapping msp.FabricMSPConfig) into a typed MSP struct for reuse of CA certs and related material.",
    "U8 verifies certificate issuance by parsing the PEM x509 certificate and validating its chain/signature against the target MSP's root and intermediate CAs; on success the certificate is appended to the revocation_list of the FabricMSPConfig and the config is re-marshaled; on failure a descriptive error is returned and the config is left unmodified.",
    "Error handling follows Go idioms with wrapped errors (fmt.Errorf with %w) carrying org/group/policy context; no panics on malformed input.",
    "Testing uses table-driven unit tests per function with existing or newly generated test fixtures (sample config transactions, MSP certs/CRLs), plus Go example tests (Example... functions) updated to demonstrate every new capability as required by the acceptance criteria."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "Work is performed by a single experienced Go developer already familiar with Hyperledger Fabric channel configuration structures and the repository layout.",
    "The development environment (Go toolchain, linting, CI pipeline) and the existing configtx library with its base types and test fixtures are already in place; this increment only adds the new capabilities.",
    "Estimates include design, implementation, unit tests, example-test updates, and documentation for the increment, but exclude external code review, project management, release packaging, and publication of external docs beyond GoDoc/README.",
    "The 'config.Organization' type referenced in U1 and the MSP types referenced in U7 already exist or will be minimally extended as part of this increment.",
    "Acceptance references to 'example tests' mean the repository's Go example tests that must compile and pass.",
    "All estimates are effort in work-hours, not elapsed calendar time."
  ],
  "activities": [
    {
      "activity_id": "A1",
      "activity_name": "Requirements analysis and codebase familiarization",
      "activity_description": "Analyze user stories U1-U8, map each to concrete API functions, and review the existing configtx package, protobuf types (cb.Config, ConfigGroup, policies, msp.FabricMSPConfig), and current helpers/tests to identify extension points.",
      "most_likely_effort": 6
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A2",
      "activity_name": "API design and interface definition",
      "activity_description": "Define function signatures for org/MSP retrieval, policy add/remove for application, orderer, and consortium scopes, add-org functions, and certificate revocation; define error-handling, validation, mod_policy, and version-handling conventions.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A3",
      "activity_name": "U1: Retrieve organization configuration helpers",
      "activity_description": "Implement functions to retrieve an application, orderer, or consortium org's configuration as a config.Organization by unmarshaling the relevant config group (policies, MSP, anchor peers, orderer endpoints). Includes table-driven unit tests.",
      "most_likely_effort": 16
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A4",
      "activity_name": "U2: Application org and group policy add/remove",
      "activity_description": "Implement functions to add and remove policies on an application org and on the Application group, producing a modifiable config transaction. Includes unit tests for add, remove, overwrite, and not-found cases.",
      "most_likely_effort": 12
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A5",
      "activity_name": "U3: Orderer org and group policy add/remove",
      "activity_description": "Implement functions to add and remove policies on an orderer org and on the Orderer group. Includes unit tests; reuses policy conversion helpers from U2.",
      "most_likely_effort": 10
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A6",
      "activity_name": "U4: Consortium-level policy add/remove",
      "activity_description": "Implement functions to add and remove policies on a consortium org, the Consortiums group, and an individual consortium group. Includes unit tests covering all three scopes.",
      "most_likely_effort": 12
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A7",
      "activity_name": "U5: Add orderer organization function",
      "activity_description": "Implement a fit-for-purpose function that builds a complete orderer org ConfigGroup (MSP, policies, orderer endpoints, mod_policy) from an Organization argument and adds it to the channel config's Orderer group, returning the mutated config transaction. Includes unit tests.",
      "most_likely_effort": 12
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A8",
      "activity_name": "U6: Add application organization function",
      "activity_description": "Implement a fit-for-purpose function that builds an application org ConfigGroup (MSP, policies, anchor peers, mod_policy) and adds it to the Application group, returning the mutated config transaction. Includes unit tests.",
      "most_likely_effort": 10
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A9",
      "activity_name": "U7: Retrieve MSP configuration for an organization",
      "activity_description": "Implement a function that extracts and unmarshals an org's msp.MSPConfig/FabricMSPConfig from a config transaction for reuse of CA certs and related material. Includes unit tests for found/not-found and malformed-MSP cases.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A10",
      "activity_name": "U8: Revoke certificate with MSP issuance verification",
      "activity_description": "Implement a function that parses a PEM x509 certificate, verifies it was issued by the target org's MSP (chain/signature check against root and intermediate CAs), appends it to the FabricMSPConfig revocation_list, and re-marshals the config; returns an error for certs not issued by the MSP. Includes unit tests for success and failure paths.",
      "most_likely_effort": 16
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A11",
      "activity_name": "Example tests update (acceptance criteria)",
      "activity_description": "Update and extend the repository's Go example tests to demonstrate usage of every new capability from U1-U8, ensuring examples compile, run, and pass.",
      "most_likely_effort": 12
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A12",
      "activity_name": "Documentation",
      "activity_description": "Write GoDoc comments for all exported functions and types, and update README/package documentation with short usage guidance for the new helpers.",
      "most_likely_effort": 6
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A13",
      "activity_name": "Integration, CI verification, and defect fixing",
      "activity_description": "Run full test suite, vetting and linting, fix integration issues across the new functions (chaining of config transaction updates), and verify end-to-end flows combining retrieval, policy updates, org addition, MSP retrieval, and revocation.",
      "most_likely_effort": 10
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A14",
      "activity_name": "Self code review and refactoring",
      "activity_description": "Perform a final self-review pass for consistency, naming, error messages, and duplication; refactor shared logic (e.g., policy conversion helpers) and confirm all acceptance criteria are met.",
      "most_likely_effort": 6
    }
  ],
  "reported_total": {
    "most_likely_effort": 144
  }
}

